WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.985
An AI inference cluster is a coordinated set of compute resources that serves requests to

00:00:04.985 --> 00:00:06.040
already trained models.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.970
Those resources may be several computers, several GPU servers,

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.760
or a mix of GPU and CPU nodes behind a shared interface.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.595
The cluster accepts prompts or other inputs, selects an appropriate model and worker,

00:00:16.595 --> 00:00:20.320
performs inference, and returns results while managing competing demand.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.710
Its purpose is operational capacity: supporting more models, users,

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:27.240
or simultaneous jobs than one serving process can handle reliably.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:31.040
It helps to distinguish three scales.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.060
A single workstation has one operating-system instance and is the simplest system to

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.160
secure and maintain.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.460
A multi-GPU server scales up inside one machine, sharing memory pathways, storage,

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.380
and management while gaining additional accelerator capacity.

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:44.680
A distributed cluster scales out across networked nodes.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.560
Scaling out can increase aggregate throughput, isolate model roles, and add resilience,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.170
but it introduces routing, queueing, network dependencies, configuration drift,

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.960
and failure modes that do not exist on one host.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.190
Use cases range from a local lab that shares a few models across a team,

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.586
to a small business hosting private assistants,

00:01:00.586 --> 00:01:03.255
to an enterprise platform serving many applications.

00:01:03.255 --> 00:01:07.210
Hybrid designs can keep sensitive inference on premises while bursting approved workloads

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:08.235
to cloud capacity.

00:01:08.235 --> 00:01:11.155
The design should follow data sensitivity, latency, cost,

00:01:11.155 --> 00:01:15.265
and availability requirements rather than a desire to imitate the largest deployment.

00:01:15.265 --> 00:01:19.850
A cluster does not combine unrelated GPUs into one unlimited pool automatically,

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.765
and it does not guarantee faster individual responses.

00:01:22.765 --> 00:01:26.875
It adds capacity only when software can place work effectively and the network, storage,

00:01:26.875 --> 00:01:28.980
and operations model support that placement.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.445
Treat the cluster as a distributed service, not a pile of accelerators.

00:01:32.445 --> 00:01:35.680
Its value comes from coordinated behavior that remains measurable, secure,

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:36.470
and supportable.
